  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 244 PM EDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\n- Slight chance for rain Tonight and Tomorrow\n\n Current radar has light precipitation streaming through lower \nMichigan this afternoon. Most of if is bifurcating the region, with \na band of scattered showers moving north of the I 96 corridor and a \nband moving along and south of the I 94 corridor. As most of the \nregion is dry, it will take a bit to moisten up the lower levels for \nany rain to accumulate. Any precipitation will be falling from a \ncloud deck around 5 to 10KFT. Soundings show fairly dry air above\nand below, so any rain falling will have to saturate the lower \nlevels. All this is to say, with a lack of moisture, and while \nthere is scattered showers possible, any rain that does fall will \nbe light at best through the early evening. More moisture will \nadvect in tonight, mainly after 10 PM, however while there is an \nincrease, it remains sparse and all in told, total QPF will \nstruggle to be measurable in most locations.\n\n  A weak, divergent trough will be squeezing its way through the \nupper level ridge through tomorrow. and while there is some mid \nlevel moisture which will result in some clouds, the divergent \npattern and dry lower levels will keep most, if not all, \nprecipitation at bay. \n\n- Warm and wet weather ahead\n\n As stated in previous discussions, the pattern is changing. A \ndeep upper trough will trek across the CONUS early week, making \nits way into the Great Lakes region on Tuesday. A warm conveyor \nbelt will bring moisture up from the Gulf out ahead of this \nsystem, and as it moves into the Great Lakes the models continue \nto have it become more positively tilted which indicates a more \nprogressive, fast moving, nature. Latest NAEFS ensembles have \nPWATS upwards of 1.25 inches which is highly anomalous for this \ntime of year. CAPEs remain fairly low so thunder is not expected. \nHowever, give the fast, moist nature of this system, a period of \nheavy rainfall are not out of the question. With that said, there \nis a concern for leaf clogged storm drains Tuesday into Wednesday \nmorning. While it is fairly far out, there is the potential of 0.5\nto 1.0 of rainfall in a short period of time. \n\n Warmer than normal temperatures will continue with highs in the \nupper 60s to low 70s through midweek. While temperatures should drop \ninto the low 60s through the latter half of the week, overall \ntemperatures will be 10 to 15 degrees above normal. \n\n- Chances for rain midweek onward\n\nBeyond the above mentioned system, the models diverge on a\nconsensus beyond midweek. However, there is a signal for \nsuccessive waves which could allow for periods of showers to be \npossible, currently only a 20 to 30 percent chance.",
